2C.  the two ways the verdict flips, which is why it cannot be quoted either way
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    distance error < 12% (TRGB/Cepheid/direct),  8 galaxies: a_0 = 9.0778e-11 +- 0.104 dex   canonical -0.10s   alt -0.72s
    distance error >= 12% (Hubble flow),         15 galaxies: a_0 = 5.6774e-11 +- 0.070 dex   canonical -2.01s   alt -2.77s
  [PASS] 2C.1 AGAINST INTEREST, and it is fatal to any footing claim from this sample: splitting the SAME M/L-free galaxies on distance QUALITY moves a_0 by more than twice the separation between the two footings.  The eight galaxies with TRGB or Cepheid distances land on the canonical footing; the fifteen with Hubble-flow distances land 0.2 dex below it.  A verdict that flips on a cut choice is not a verdict   (good distances 9.078e-11 vs Hubble flow 5.677e-11: +0.204 dex, against a footing separation of 0.082 dex; on the good-distance half alt is only -0.72 sigma away)
  
    deep-tail points, all baryons                        a_0 = 7.3612e-11  (-0.104 canonical, -0.186 alt)
    outermost point, all baryons                         a_0 = 7.8158e-11  (-0.078 canonical, -0.160 alt)
    BTFR zero-point, C removed                           a_0 = 7.1557e-11  (-0.117 canonical, -0.198 alt)
    outermost point, GAS ONLY (biased +0.086 dex, h124)  a_0 = 9.0875e-11  (-0.013 canonical, -0.095 alt)   <- upper bound, not a best estimate
    spread of the THREE defensible estimators on essentially the SAME 21-23 gas-rich SPARC discs: 0.038 dex (full range), 0.016 dex (rms)
    including the gas-only upper bound as well: 0.104 dex
  [PASS] 2C.2 MY OWN SECOND FLIP, TESTED AND WITHDRAWN.  I expected estimator choice to move a_0 by more than the gap between the footings, as the distance split does.  It does not: the three defensible M/L-free estimators on these discs span only a third of the footing separation, so estimator choice is a sub-dominant systematic here and the claim is retracted.  The range only exceeds the gap if the gas-only variant is included, and h124 already showed that one is a known-biased upper bound rather than a competing estimate   (three defensible estimators span 0.038 dex (rms 0.016) against a footing separation of 0.082 dex; with the biased gas-only variant the range is 0.104 dex)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2D.  what precision WOULD decide it, and where it would have to come from
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    required TOTAL error for a 3 sigma exclusion of one footing: 0.0273 dex (6.5%)
      on the optimistic  budget the calibration floor alone is 0.0428 dex = 1.6x the whole allowance
      on the realistic   budget the calibration floor alone is 0.0819 dex = 3.0x the whole allowance
    spending the WHOLE budget on one channel: the distance scale would have to be known to 2.84%, or the HI mass scale to 0.0246 dex (5.8%)
    a balanced requirement (half the budget to calibration, half to statistics):
      distance scale to 1.41%  AND  HI mass scale to 0.0123 dex (2.9%)  AND  about 518 gas-dominated discs with resolved curves (SPARC has 23)
  [PASS] 2D.1 the answer to item 123, stated as a requirement rather than as a result: deciding between 9.36e-11 and 1.13e-10 needs a total 2.7% on an M/L-free a_0.  That means the distance scale to about 1%, the HI mass scale (flux calibration plus the helium factor plus molecular gas) to about 3%, and roughly ten times SPARC's gas-dominated sample.  None of the three exists today, and the first two are not sample-size problems   (need 0.0273 dex; optimistic floor 0.0428; need ~518 discs vs 23 in hand)

