  [PASS] 3.1 ITEM 125's OWN CRITERION IS NOT MET, and the first reason is arithmetic rather than physical: the criterion cannot be evaluated with these rungs at all.  Item 125 asked whether the M/L-free rungs agree to better than 0.05 dex; the MEDIAN QUOTED ERROR of the seven M/L-free rungs is over three times that, because the price of removing the stellar mass is a sample of gas-rich dwarfs measured by lensing shape, HI line width or post-merger debris.  Even if every central value were identical, this ladder could not demonstrate 0.05 dex agreement   (median quoted error 0.130 dex, range 0.104-0.300, against the 0.05 dex criterion)
  [PASS] 3.2 and the central values do not agree either, though only weakly: one common a_0 across the seven is rejected at p = 0.011, an intrinsic spread of 0.133 dex by item 100's own moment estimator and 0.176 dex by maximum likelihood.  AGAINST INTEREST IN BOTH DIRECTIONS -- that is a 2-3 sigma inconsistency, not a decisive one, and it is essentially the SAME 0.16 dex item 100 found with the stellar M/L still in.  Removing the mass-to-light ratio neither collapsed the ladder nor made it worse; it left it where it was   (chi2 16.6/6 (p = 0.011); intrinsic 0.133 dex moment / 0.176 dex ML, against item 100's 0.156 dex and item 125's 0.05 dex criterion)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3A.  what the residual is organised by, now that it is not organised by Upsilon
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    raw correlation of log a_0 with the residual Upsilon leverage across the seven: r = +0.741 -- but
    with N = 7 and levers spanning only 0.00 to 0.18, and with the leverage
    collinear with which SYSTEM each rung is, that correlation cannot be read as an Upsilon organiser.  The
    physical test is whether a COMMON error in Upsilon could reconcile the rungs: fit log a_0 = mu + lever x d.
      best-fit common Upsilon shift d log Upsilon = +2.48 dex (a factor 302 in the stellar
      mass-to-light ratio), which does move chi2 from 16.6 to 3.4
      but a BINARY 'is this a SPARC rung' indicator, which contains no Upsilon at all, does the same job:
      chi2 16.6 -> 4.5.  The leverage column is a proxy for which sample a rung came from,
      because 3 of the 7 rungs have leverage exactly zero and all three SPARC rungs sit near 0.17.
  [PASS] 3A.0 the stellar mass-to-light ratio CANNOT be the organiser any more.  It formally fits -- but only at a shift of +2.5 dex, a factor of three hundred in the stellar M/L against stellar populations' 0.5 +- 0.1 (0.09 dex) -- and a binary sample-membership indicator carrying no Upsilon whatsoever absorbs the same chi2, which shows the fit is sample membership in disguise.  AGAINST INTEREST: my first version of this check asserted that Upsilon leaves the chi2 behind.  It does not; it is degenerate with the sample label, which is a weaker statement and the one that is true   (required shift +2.48 dex (factor 302) vs the 0.09 dex stellar-population uncertainty; chi2 16.6 -> 3.4 on the leverage, -> 4.5 on a bare SPARC/not-SPARC flag; 3 of 7 rungs have zero leverage)
      velocity class HI line width  : N = 2, median log a_0 = -10.371 (-0.342 dex from canonical)
      velocity class resolved RC    : N = 4, median log a_0 = -10.139 (-0.110 dex from canonical)
      velocity class weak lensing   : N = 1, median log a_0 = -10.200 (-0.171 dex from canonical)
    SPARC resolved rotation curves minus HI line widths: +0.238 dex
  [PASS] 3A.1 the residual's new organiser is the VELOCITY MEASUREMENT.  The rungs built on resolved rotation curves sit about 0.24 dex above the rungs built on unresolved HI line widths -- the direction and roughly the size of the width bias item 124 measured directly on galaxies whose stellar masses ARE known (+0.25 dex per dex of mass in ALFALFA against +0.07 in SPARC).  Stated with its own limitation: this is a comparison of 3 rungs against 2, so it is a diagnosis consistent with an independently measured bias, not an independent detection   (SPARC resolved minus HI width +0.238 dex, on 3 rungs vs 2; the independently measured ALFALFA width bias runs +0.246 dex/dex of mass where SPARC's resolved curves run +0.070 +- 0.033)
    and the outlier that is neither: the tidal dwarfs sit -0.628 dex below the resolved-curve rungs on RESOLVED kinematics, so their offset is not a width bias -- it is item 46's liability (equilibrium one orbit after a merger), and it cannot be cut without cutting on the answer

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3B.  the sub-ladder of resolved kinematics only, and why it is one measurement and not four
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    the three SPARC rungs alone: mean 7.435e-11, total spread 0.016 dex, intrinsic 0.000 dex
    BUT they are not three measurements: R1 and R2 share 21 of 21 galaxies, R1 and R3 share 15 of 20, and all three use the same distances, the same inclinations and the same HI mass scale
  [PASS] 3B.1 the three SPARC rungs DO agree to better than 0.05 dex -- their rms is 0.016 dex -- and that is NOT a second law, because R2's galaxies are a SUBSET of R1's and R3 shares three quarters of them, and all three use the same distances, the same inclinations and the same hydrogen mass scale.  Recorded here explicitly so that this 0.016 dex cannot later be mistaken for the item-125 criterion being met: their concordance tests the estimator, not nature, and three estimators on one sample is one rung   (rms of the three 0.016 dex (moment intrinsic 0.000 once the shared floor is charged), but R1-R2 overlap 21 of 21 galaxies and R1-R3 overlap 15 of 20)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3C.  the dynamic range the M/L-free ladder actually spans
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    baryonic mass covered by all seven M/L-free rungs: log M_b = 8.15 to 11.00 = 2.8 decades
    local baryonic acceleration where one exists:       log g_bar = -14.80 to -11.26 = 3.5 decades
    drop the one rung whose own a_0 is unstable at 0.30 dex (KiDS shape-only): 2.5 decades of mass
    keep only the three rungs that carry the measurement (the SPARC gas-rich discs): 1.1 decades, log M_b 8.37 to 9.51
  [PASS] 3C.1 the structural reason item 125 could not have closed, which is worth more than the failed criterion.  M/L-freedom and dynamic range are in DIRECT CONFLICT: the only way to remove the stellar mass is to select gas-rich systems, gas-rich systems are low-mass, and low-mass systems all sit at the same acceleration.  The item promised one acceleration measured five ways over nine decades of mass; the M/L-free ladder that actually exists spans one decade in the rungs that carry it and 2.5 decades if every usable rung is admitted, and every one of those rungs is a gas-rich dwarf   (load-bearing SPARC rungs span 1.1 decades of M_b against the item's nine; all seven span 2.8, and 2.5 once the 0.30 dex KiDS rung is dropped)

