5.  MUTATION CONTROLS
========================================================================================================================
    M1: multiplying one of the three concordant SPARC rungs by 2.0 raises their intrinsic spread from 0.000 to 0.084 dex
  [PASS] M1 the agreement of section 3B is falsifiable: injecting a factor-2 error into one of the three concordant SPARC rungs takes their intrinsic spread from under the 0.05 dex threshold to far above it   (0.000 -> 0.084 dex on a x2.0 injection)
    M2: 4000 synthetic ladders drawn from ONE a_0 with these same quoted errors give
        moment intrinsic spread: median 0.000, 95th percentile 0.188 dex -- observed 0.133
        chi2 about a common a_0:  median 5.4, 95th percentile 12.8 -- observed 16.6
  [PASS] M2 the null is run, and it CORRECTS MY OWN CHECK 3.2 in part.  Item 100's moment estimator of the intrinsic spread is too noisy at N = 7 with these error bars to detect the observed 0.13 dex: its own null reaches 0.18 dex at the 95th percentile.  The chi2 does clear its null, so the inconsistency is real but it rests on the chi2 and NOT on the intrinsic-spread number, which must therefore be quoted as an estimate and never as a detection.  Item 100's headline 0.156 dex inherits exactly the same weakness   (observed chi2 16.6 above the null's 95th percentile 12.8, but observed intrinsic 0.133 dex BELOW the moment estimator's null 95th percentile 0.188)
    M3: synthetic g_obs built from the R1 g_bar values at a_0 = 4.0x canonical = 3.744e-10 is recovered as 3.744e-10 (+0.00000 dex)
        and AGAINST INTEREST, a factor-4 error in a_0 registers as only 5.8 sigma against this measurement's coherent floor -- which is how weak an M/L-free a_0 currently is, not a failure of the control
  [PASS] M3 the estimator is not a fixed point and follows the data: an injected a_0 four times canonical is recovered as four times canonical to better than 0.001 dex   (injected 3.7440e-10, recovered 3.7440e-10 (+0.00000 dex); it would register at 5.8 sigma)
  [PASS] M4 turning the kernel off (nu = 1, g_obs set equal to g_bar) must leave no a_0 that fits, since no boosted kernel can reproduce an unboosted curve   (nu=1 synthetic returns a_0 = nan)
  [PASS] M5 the only covariance this script builds is checked for positive-definiteness rather than for its diagonal, which is the discipline that caught the Brouwer index-ordering bug earlier in the hunt   (min eigenvalue 1.090e-02)

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- ITEMS 123 AND 125
========================================================================================================================

  ITEM 123 -- NEITHER FOOTING IS DECIDED, and the reason is not sample size.
    The best M/L-free measurement in the hunt is the SPARC deep tail with stars supplying under 20% of g_bar:
    a_0 = 7.361e-11 +- 0.065 dex statistical, 0.104 dex with the coherent
    calibration floor rebuilt here.  That is -1.0 sigma from
    the canonical footing and -1.8 sigma from the alt footing:
    neither is excluded at 3 sigma.  One further fact forbids quoting it either way: splitting the SAME galaxies
    on distance quality moves a_0 by 0.20 dex -- 2.5 times the whole gap between the
    footings -- with the good-distance half landing ON the canonical value (9.08e-11) and the Hubble-flow half
    0.2 dex below it.  (My second candidate flip, estimator choice, was tested and WITHDRAWN: the three
    defensible M/L-free estimators span only 0.038 dex, well inside the gap.)
    The precision needed is 0.0273 dex total, i.e. the distance scale to about 1.4%, the HI mass scale to
    about 3%, and roughly 518 gas-dominated discs with resolved rotation curves against SPARC's
    23.  Removing the stellar mass-to-light ratio does not remove the calibration problem: it trades it
    for the hydrogen mass scale, whose leverage on this estimator is 1.11, about eight times the
    stellar leverage (0.15) that the cut removed.  That trade is the reason the floor does not fall.

  ITEM 125 -- THE HUNT DID NOT FIND A SECOND LAW.  The M/L-free ladder is not worse than the one it replaced,
    and it is not better; it is the same size, for a different reason.
    Seven M/L-free rungs.  The criterion cannot even be evaluated: their median quoted error is 0.13
    dex, three times the 0.05 dex agreement item 125 asked for, so identical central values could not have
    demonstrated it.  The central values are in mild tension anyway -- chi2 16.6 on 6 d.o.f.,
    p = 0.011 -- with an intrinsic spread of 0.133 dex by item 100's moment estimator and 0.176
    dex by maximum likelihood, against item 100's 0.156 dex.  The null in control M2 says the moment
    estimator cannot detect that at N = 7, so the tension rests on the chi2 and the intrinsic-spread number is an
    estimate, never a detection -- which retires item 100's 0.156 dex as a detection too.
    The organiser has CHANGED.  It is no longer the stellar mass-to-light ratio: 3 of the seven rungs have
    exactly zero M/L leverage, the best common Upsilon shift is an impossible +2.5 dex (a factor
    302 against stellar populations' 0.09 dex), and a bare sample-membership flag carrying no
    Upsilon absorbs the same chi2 (16.6 -> 4.5 against 3.4), so what the leverage column
    fits is which catalogue a rung came from.
    It is now the VELOCITY MEASUREMENT -- resolved rotation curves sit +0.24 dex above
    unresolved HI line widths, the direction and size of the width bias item 124 measured independently -- with
    the tidal dwarfs 0.63 dex below everything on an equilibrium
    assumption rather than on an estimator.
    The three SPARC rungs agree to 0.016 dex rms, and that must NOT be read as the law closing: R2's
    galaxies are a subset of R1's, R3 shares three quarters of them, and all three share one distance scale, one
    inclination convention and one hydrogen calibration.  Three estimators on one sample is one rung.
    The structural finding is the durable one.  M/L-freedom and dynamic range are in direct conflict -- the only
    way to drop the stellar mass is to select gas-rich systems, which are low-mass, which all sit at the same
    acceleration.  The load-bearing M/L-free rungs span 1.1 decades of baryonic mass and the
    whole usable ladder 2.5, not the nine the item promised, and every rung is a gas-rich dwarf.

  ONE CORRECTION THIS ITEM OWES THE LEDGER, against my own workflow's earlier headline.
    Item 100's closing sentence -- 'the two measurements that do not lean on a stellar M/L agree to 0.08 dex' --
    is WITHDRAWN.  Neither of the two was M/L-free.  The deep-tail rung carries a measured leverage of -0.647
    (h121a) and the KiDS dwarf lens stack carries -1.046 (h111-A, where a deep-MOND lensing a_0 is degenerate with
    the assumed baryonic mass at exponent one, not two).  The 0.08 dex agreement was additionally an artefact of
    the +0.10 dex estimator bias that item 102 found in item 25.  Both halves of the sentence fail.


